[Music]  it was a scene of catastrophic  carnage a busy tram full of commuters  derailed with devastating consequences  these are the seven who died that day  their families have waited over four  years to find out why  we didn't even get to say goodbye that's  that's the hardest thing  is we didn't get to say goodbye  danielle's grandad philip logan was a  bricklayer  that morning it was raining and he  almost stayed at home  i just wish that he'd stuck to his guns  and not got on that tram that morning  but he went to work he went to work and  he didn't even make it to work  investigators said the crash was fast  and violent  their analysis shows the tram overturned  because it went too quickly around this  bend  here's what happened the tram set off  just before  6 am exiting the sandylands tunnels it  was going 48 miles an hour  within the 50 speed limit just before  the corner that limit drops to 12 miles  an hour but the tram was doing  45 nearly four times the speed limit  seconds later it derailed reports  concluded  the driver was probably in a micro sleep  at the time  this is where the tram overturned it was  something  nobody ever expected to happen despite  the fact there have been  previous late-breaking incidents at this  exact spot  including one just 10 days before the  accident  changes have been made there's extra  signage  a driver alertness system and automatic  brakes if trams speed  but the families feel frustrated the  inquest didn't dig  deeper with every single breath  that i have i'm never going to let this  lie  that man and those six other people  deserve justice  their lives have already been lost they  don't they don't get to continue their  life and their journeys  but i do those left behind  are struggling to move forward their  heartbreak and hunt for answers  continues laura bundock sky news

